"Distinguishes requirement types by how their fulfillment and absence affect customer satisfaction.\n\nUse when: Discuss why basic expectations and delightful extras need different treatment. Interpret customer requirement evidence before prioritizing an offer.\n\nAvoid when: The team needs observed statistical curves or a precise economic ranking of features.\n\nVisual grammar: Axes conceptually relate fulfillment to satisfaction. Distinct relationship curves illustrate basic, performance and attractive needs. Requirement notes identify the segment and evidence behind classification.\n\nBuild: Choose a customer segment and define the requirement precisely. Collect evidence about reactions to presence and absence. Classify cautiously and discuss how context and expectations may change.\n\nCheck: Conceptual curves are not presented as fitted measurements. Absence and fulfillment are both considered. Requirement categories are scoped to a segment and time.\n\nPitfalls: Calling an internally exciting feature a customer delighter. Assuming needs remain in the same category indefinitely.\n\nExample: A guesthouse distinguishes clean rooms as expected, faster check-in as performance-related and an unusually helpful local welcome as potentially attractive.\n\nAlternatives: House of Quality, Value Proposition Canvas."
